module nupp.compiler.cli.aot

--[[
`nupp aot`: what an `@aot` function compiles to.

An `@aot` body is compiled somewhere a reader cannot see, so `--emit ir` shows
the verified IR, `--emit c` shows the generated C, `--emit simd` shows what a
`@simd` loop was rewritten into as ordinary Nupp, `--emit spirv` writes the
native WGPU module, `--emit wgsl` shows the browser WebGPU integer artifact,
`--emit asm` shows the instructions that C became, and `--emit binding` shows
the module that stands in front of it. Reading the C is how you tell a mask
stack from a branch, and how you check that the loop you thought was
register-resident is.

`--emit asm` is where that stops being a guess. The C compiler is the last thing
between a lowering decision and the machine, and until it was readable from here
the only way to see what it did was to compile the emitted C by hand and run a
disassembler over it -- four commands, typed differently every time, producing an
answer that could not be compared with last week's. It takes `--function` and
`--features`, so what is asked for is one body at one tier, and it counts the
listing by kind so that two runs of the same kernel can be put side by side.

With no `--emit`, one line per function says what it lowered to: the species
each `@simd` loop runs in, or that it runs scalar. Whether a loop runs in lanes
is the source's to say, so there is nothing here to check for -- a `@simd` loop
that cannot run in lanes already fails the build, with the construct that
refused it.
]]
const {type Target} = require("nupp.compiler.aot.target")
const {type Toolchain} = require("nupp.compiler.build.aot")
const {type Claim} = require("nupp.compiler.aot.instructions")
const {type Listing} = require("nupp.compiler.aot.instructions")

local usageError = require("nupp.compiler.cli.error").forCommand("aot")
local report = require("nupp.compiler.cli.report")
local cliModule = require("nupp.cli")

